## Why This Is Asked
This question tests your ability to design a practical, privacy-preserving NLP feature for multilingual chat environments. It evaluates understanding of on-device processing constraints, entity normalization challenges, and real-world deployment considerations for streaming platforms.

## Key Concepts
- Hybrid detection: lexicon-based pattern matching + lightweight ML classifier
- On-device inference with privacy-by-design architecture
- Multilingual text processing and code-switching tolerance
- Active learning for continuous data labeling and improvement
- Phased evaluation: offline metrics, shadow testing, staged rollout

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: hybrid entity detection approach
function extractEntity(tokens, lexicon, model) {
  // Fast lexicon lookup first
  const matches = lexiconLookup(tokens);
  if (matches.length > 0) {
    return normalizeEntities(matches);
  }
  // Fallback to lightweight classifier
  const predictions = model.predict(tokens);
  return filterByConfidence(predictions);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question explores the challenge of acronym expansion and text normalization in a multilingual, on-device environment with stringent latency, memory, and privacy requirements.

## Key Concepts
- On-device NLP with privacy-preserving inference
- Lightweight hybrid approach combining rule-based and ML methods
- Multilingual text normalization and tokenization strategies
- End-to-end latency budgeting and performance evaluation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Implementation sketch: dictionary lookup for abbreviations
const abbreviationDict = {
  'eod': 'end of day',
  'fyi': 'for your information',
  'ceo': 'chief executive officer'
};

function normalizeText(text, lang = 'en') {
  // Tokenize and expand abbreviations
  const tokens = text.toLowerCase().split(/\s+/);
  const normalized = tokens.map(token => 
    abbreviationDict[token] || token
  );
  return normalized.join(' ');
}
```

